+# ECON-02B2d18a — Generic machine execution foundation (local prototype gate)
+
+**Project:** Single Galaxy original game (not the separate EVEmu audit).
+**Date:** November 1, 2026.
+**Exact source baseline:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d17_Generic_Retirement_Multi_Destination_Source_Tests_2026-11-01.zip`.
+**Status:** New deterministic C++17 runtime component and regression tests; **NOT YET integrated into the authoritative colony PersistentWorld, relay, NPC scheduler or graphical runtime**. Existing d17 player-facing behavior is unchanged. GitHub remains a source repository, not a development hub.
+
+## Implementation
+
+1. `FactoryExecution.hpp/.cpp` introduces data-driven installed machine instances. Equipment blueprints are authored through the existing physically validated `FactoryLayout`; recipes and five-tier production are executed by the existing `QuantifiedLine` engine. No per-product machine whitelist is introduced.
+2. Blueprint placement is **not** installation. `install_machine` debits one existing manufactured construction module from an external module account. `install_conveyor` requires installed endpoints and debits one module for its link plus one for each spatial route cell. Receipt identifiers are unique and serialized. Existing material custody must be supplied by an authorized caller; this component does not issue inventory or construct NPCs.
+3. Each installed machine has separate, finite input, product, scrap and reserved-WIP quantities. Production conserves material grams, including scrap. A machine's total internal mass cannot exceed its tier-scaled buffer. A shutdown allows reserved WIP to complete but forbids new batches and inbound material.
+4. Installed directed conveyor links carry only their declared material at their installed, per-tick rate. Staged/partially dismantled links do not transfer. Uninstalled blueprints do not carry stock. Transfers are validated for source inventory, target capacity, and defined ports.
+5. Retirement order reuses `FactoryRetirementPlanner`: conveyor links must come down before their source/destination machines, and consumers precede their suppliers. Removing one link cell or machine returns its intact construction module into an existing bounded NPC-cargo counter. Real colony NPC identity, route and depot ownership checks are intentionally left to the authoritative host adapter, **not asserted here**.
+6. `checkpoint()`/`restore()` provide a canonical deterministic v1 runtime serialization. They bind to the exact layout-and-recipe configuration, preserve WIP, link removal progress, unique installation receipts, counters, and inventories, and reject noncanonical or malformed restore inputs. This is *structural* validation, not a replacement for the cryptographic hashes or SQLite event/receipt audit of the live game.
+7. New `ECON02B2d18FactoryExecutionTests.cpp` exercises installation refusal without stock, installed-link-only transfers, two different production recipes, mid-cycle restore, shutdown, invalid inventory, material conservation through dismantling, malformed receipts, Tier 5 scaling, and config mismatch. All material movement in this prototype remains explicit and bounded.
+
+## Boundaries retained
+
+- The preexisting primary processor and finisher continue operating exactly as before, through the main `Simulation` and its SQLite journal. This code introduces **no competing world writer**.
+- The `FactoryExecution` stores are local, deterministic domain objects: their external source/destination `Store` references must be mapped to real ship/warehouse/NPC custody by the host before calling from gameplay. Passing a fabricated account to this library is not a game permission.
+- The runtime doesn't create population, power, maintenance, workforce, transport vehicles, or space/distant routes; it calls the quantified machine's existing power/staff/maintenance gates and respects spatially authored conveyor paths.
+- One manufactured construction module per newly installed machine is a provisional machine-cost rule. Tiers currently scale recipe batch size and internal capacity; tier-dependent multi-module dismantling needs an authoritative progressive salvage model.
+- Synchronous per-tick routing in this prototype is not yet a production-ready massively parallel/offline scheduling implementation. Very large graphs need a partitioned, bounded batch scheduler.
+- `FactoryLayout` already prevents cyclic conveyor networks. The independent retirement planner also rejects invalid dependency DAGs. General world command and lifecycle barriers remain future integration gates.
+
+## To close ECON-02B2d18 fully
+
+Integrate `FactoryExecution` into `Simulation` with unique site-local machine IDs and versioned canonical snapshots; implement server-authorized commands for installation, material pickup/delivery, machine processing, shutdown and dismantling; record all transfers in the existing SQLite generation and resource ledgers with replay validation; add NPC crew schedule and spatial route verification; test crash timing between world commit and scheduler intent update, retained inventories, competing crews, and the authenticated colony GUI on both Linux and Windows.
+
+**No claims of complete d18 world integration, Windows validation, or NPC autonomous operation are made in this subgate.**
+
+## Actual verification of this deliverable
+
+- Full Linux CMake/Ninja build (all configured targets): **PASS**.
+- CTest cases 1–75: **75/75 PASS**.
+- CTest cases 76–110: **35/35 PASS**. Total **110/110** across two runs, including the new `ECON02B2d18_GenericFactoryExecution` test and existing Node HTTP tests with their required CLI arguments.
+- Existing offline Chromium colony crew smoke and structures smoke: **2/2 PASS**. They test unchanged old UI, not a new generic-machine panel.
+- New C++ test: installation receipts, tier-five recipe scaling, shutdown with reserved WIP, correct physical link transfers, transfer refusal, exact checkpoint restore, config mismatch, separate scrap inventories and mass conservation through dismantling: **PASS**.
+- Windows and an end-to-end generic-machine SQLite/HTTP command test: **NOT RUN** (not yet implemented).
